$\mathrm{V}^{4+}, \mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ having unpaired electrons in $d$-subshell. Therefore, there ions show $d$ - $d$ transition and form different colours. $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ does not have unpaired electrons therefore, it is colourless.

$\begin{array}{ll|ll} \hline & \text{lons} & & \begin{array}{c}  \text{Colour} \\ (\mathrm{d}-\mathrm{d} \text{ transition)} \end{array} \\ \hline \text{(A)} & \mathrm{V}^{4+} & \text{(IV)} & \text{Blue colour} \\ \hline \text{(B)} & \mathrm{Ti}^{3+} & \text{(II)} & \text{Purple} \\ \hline \text{(C)} & \mathrm{Ti}^{4+} & \text{(I)} & \text{Colourless} \\ \hline \text{(D)} & \mathrm{Ni}^{2+} & \text{(III)} & \text{Green} \\ \hline \end{array}

Thus, option (1) is correct.

